<!-- https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/package-validation/diagnostic-ids -->
<!--
  Every entry here is a difference from the baseline the packages are compared against, which is the newest release on
  nuget.org: 0.8.0. The version after it was never released, so its differences are still differences a person upgrading
  from 0.8.0 meets, and they stay here until a release of this library becomes the baseline itself.

  A window now says its stride for each axis rather than once for every axis, so what was a whole number is a pair of
  them. That is the signal it is meant to be: an engine written outside the library stops compiling rather than quietly
  walking the wrong places, and the two entries for it are what the comparison reports, one for each target framework.
  The one number still means both axes, so a window written as it always was still reads.

  An optimizer is open to code outside the library: what it remembers of a parameter between steps (MemoryOf) and puts
  back from a checkpoint (Recall) are protected members every optimizer has to write. Both were internal in 0.8.0, so no
  code outside the library could derive from Optimizer and nothing that compiled against it can break; the comparison
  cannot tell, and says so, once for each.

  The tensor seam gained ITensorBackend.FirstLargest, which a max pooling's value and its gradient are made of. An engine
  written outside the library has one more operation to write, and the comparison says so, once for each target framework:
  a meant break, named in the changelog's upgrading notes.
-->
